A||X||21||John Smith||Intermitant Idle Problems||||9||
Z||000000||John Smith||11-09-1999||02:13 PM||||Hello all,<BR>   I am hoping that someone may be able to help me out a bit with my 88<BR>Formula 5spd.<P>Problem :<P>(Please note that this situation is intermitant, several days of normal<BR>operation and then.. boom. )<P>    As closed loop temp is reached (ie.. 160 deg.) the base idle speed<BR>suddenly increases to 2000 rpm.  The throtle actually seems to stick at 2000<BR>rpm, evident during gear changes, but the throtle body plate does not appear<BR>to be sticking, and is completely shut at idle.  A code 24 (vehicle speed<BR>sensor) is set when this occurs, but only when first coming to a full stop<BR>after the initial idle jump.  At times, the idle will "very" slowly drop to<BR>normal (900 rpm) after coming to a stop (this drop occurs over approx. 1 - 2<BR>minute time period) but it resumes shortly after reaching 30 to 40 mph.<BR>Normal operation can be obtained "temporarily" after a roadside ECM reset.<P>    The high idle condition is not present with the engine running in<BR>diagnostic mode, and sometimes the idle will even return to normal after<BR>driving for an extended period, (this seems to correspond with an operating<BR>temperature equilibrium of approx. 200 deg.).  Disconnecting the IAC harness<BR>has no effect.  Disconnecting the EGR selenoid harness has no effect.  The<BR>EGR valve tests normal and no vacuum leaks were evident with several<BR>extensive detection attempts (ie..starter fluid sprayed in suspect areas).<P>The following items have been replaced but had no effect:<P>Oxygen sensor<BR>EGR Tube<BR>IAC valve<BR>IAC valve again (just to make sure I didnt get a bad one)<BR>TPS<BR>"New" Distributor<BR>Spark Plug Wires, cap, rotor<BR>Coil<BR>Fuel Filter<BR>Thermostat<P>    The intermittant nature of this thing has presented quite a challenge in<BR>its diagnosis.  Trial and error methods are no longer logical due to the<BR>expense of the remaining components that may be involved.  (ie. MAP, EGR<BR>System, VSS, ECM), so I am now considering an investment in an AutoXray<BR>scanner.<BR>If anyone could help it sure would save me an ulcer or two.<P>John Smith<P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||11-09-1999||04:47 PM||||Sounds exactly like the problem I had about a week ago except it was the first time I have ever seen this happen on my car.  I had the always present constant speed zero acceleration hesitation, but never the high rev idle then one day it happened.<P>Have no explanation for it and I also tripped the same code.  I disconnected the battery to reset the ECM and haven't had it since.  Now, it's only been around a week, but so far so good.  Have no explanations for it, but will repost if I see it again.  If you find out before me, let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Fiero||11-09-1999||05:19 PM||||Sometimes after fixing a problem specially when a code is given..it is alot easier to disconnect the battery to erase that code in the ECM because it stores it...||||reg||1
Z||000003||John Smith||11-09-1999||06:36 PM||||Mr. Boilermaker:<BR> <BR>That may be more than just a coincidence, since I also have a surging problem at constant speeds.  It would be interesting if these two symtoms were related to the same malfunction.<P>John Smith||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-09-1999||07:31 PM||||get the scanner. you'll use it more than for this. The scanner can monitor in real time with one note. Some versions of the GM ECMs can't handle realtime scanning and screw up the idle. This is an ECM problem, not related to any scanner. If you activate the scanner and the idle changes signifigantly you should be carefull about trying to drive the car, or not drive it at all.||||reg||1
Z||000006||John Smith||11-10-1999||04:30 AM||||Well, I bought an Autoxray tonight and hooked it up.  The intermittant problem was of course not evident.. I think it only likes to do it during the day, maybe temp related.  <P>I did see an abnormal fluctuation of the idle after it had set for a few minutes.  A couple of sudden bursts would occur up to about 1800 rpm for no apparent reason.  The IAC counts were normally around 9, and would burst up to about 33.  The MAP voltage also fluctuated during this burst from a normal 1.25 volts to around 1.5.  I have no idea what this would indicate.  <P>I will try again tomorrow, and hope for a fairly warm day.<P>BTW, I submitted the exact same text as my original post here to both the "Fiero List" and "Fiero Tech-net".  I have recieved absolutely NO replies from either list, personally or on the lists themselves.  <BR>The Fiero List seems way too busy with their pi$$ing contest about 86.5 GT's to address someones stupid problem.<P>John Smith||||reg||1
Z||000007||Thomas||11-10-1999||08:00 AM||||Have you check the Air Temperature Sensor?<BR>The Problem of high idle can be : You get Strange air into the Airmanifold. <BR>That can came from cracking ECR tube or seal.<BR>Cracking Air Manifold seal.<BR>Look for this. Maybe this is the problem.||||reg||10
Z||000008||John Smith||11-10-1999||07:07 PM||||Does anyone know what the "Spark Advance" reading at idle on the AutoXray scanner should be for an 88 V6?||||reg||1
Z||000009||John Smith||11-11-1999||03:20 AM||||I still have not been able to test during a warm day, since a cold front here prevents it.  I am sure that whatever is causing the rpm burst is also responsible for the sticking high rpm symptom.  Several repeated bursts were observed that coincided with the cooling fan activation, so I think I may be on to something.  The idle only seems to malfunction after driving for some time.  If it were some electical component on its way south, I would guess that heat would play an important part in its abnormal operation.<P>  I hope no one minds my ramble, but I still have hopes that someone has encountered something of this nature and may offer some ideas.<P>John Smith||||reg||1
Z||000010||John Smith||11-13-1999||08:07 PM||||The sticking has not reappeared even with considerable driving during a fairly warm day.  The idle bursts are still present, occuring aprox. 15 to 30 sec. after the car has come to a stop.  I have concluded that it is probably just an intake lifter gummed up a bit.  So, I wont bother you any further.  BTW, I did not notice a way to un-register from this forum. This is obviously the reason it advertises to have over 500 members. I am sure that I am not the only one to find this inaccurate figure to be quite misleading.<P>Regards,<BR>          John Smith||||reg||1
Z||000011||broke||11-14-1999||08:00 AM||||John Smith, a lot of registered people check this Forum regularly but do not respond to requests...we are only apparent when we need help...but the topics covered show us how to do things without asking.   Don't put down a good forum!||||reg||14
Z||000012||batboy||11-14-1999||01:43 PM||||John raises an interesting point about not being able to unsubscribe to the Forum, if this is true. On the other hand, we are not in a pissing contest with any other Fiero bulletin boards to see which one is more popular or has the most members.<P>This Forum is fun, informative, and sometimes amusing (the general discussion section anyway). I've been helped many times by numerous people and have bought several parts from Forum members. I've even had a member send me a part for free. I hope that I've provided some help or info to people in return. I get emails from Forum members all the time asking questions about V8 Fieros (since I have one). Lots of nice people here that truly love Fieros.<P>John, from what I've seen, only a small percentage of members have any expertise in the complicated electronics troubleshooting questions like what you were asking. I know I didn't have a clue about half the stuff you were talking about, so I'm assuming many others are like me too.<P>There have been a couple questions that I've asked that didn't get a definitive answer too, but the vast majority of my questions have gotten great advice. I think most of us here are hobbyists or weekend shade tree mechanics (not professionals) that work on our cars to save shop labor charges and to have the satisfaction of fixing our little pride and joy with your own two hands (and maybe a set of tools too).<P>I suspect that there hundreds of other nonmembers that occasionally refer to this Forum for info. Lots of regular members are probably "lurkers" too, good people that rarely respond, but make use of the Forum anyway.<P>Well, I just noticed I was doing some major rambling. Anyway, my point is: this Forum is a FREE service. Sometimes you get a great bargain and sometimes you get just what you paid for, which of course is nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000013||hefty||11-14-1999||02:11 PM||||Being new to Fiero's, and searching the web for info, have found this to be the BEST scorce for information. Not to mention the people seem really cool. I don't post a lot of messages, but I do check the Forum daily (it is a sickness that I associate with my love of my '87 GT ). Thanks for every ones participation.||||reg||1
Z||000014||William Federle||11-14-1999||02:50 PM||||I think that this is the best source of <BR>information for fiero owners who work on<BR>their own cars that there is in the <BR>internet.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000015||ka4nkf||11-14-1999||03:25 PM||||I Love This Forum, I Check Up On It About Six Times A Day. This Is The Best Thing That Ever Happened For Fiero Lovers, Also It Seems To Me That There Are A Lot Of Nice Men And Women On This Forum. Lets Keep It Going.<BR>Thanks To Cliff.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Bill Osmer||11-14-1999||06:52 PM||||I, too, am addicted to this forum - I check it several times a day. I don't always leave a message - too many other good people that can contribute, too. However, I sure get enthused about the Fiero every time I start reading. I've been able to get to problems/replacements MUCH faster because usually someone has been there before me. GREAT FORUM!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000017||Bill Osmer||11-14-1999||06:54 PM||||HEY!!! I just noticed I've reached "Member" status! Today I am a man!!||||reg||1
Z||000018||Patrick||11-15-1999||12:12 AM||||<BR>As a regular here almost from the very beginning of this forum, I've got to say that I find John Smith's comments earlier in this thread to be unnecessary, unwelcome, and uncalled for.<P>This is the only thread that he has ever been involved with at this forum, and for some reason he felt the need to complain that there was no way to un-register. This is something that I doubt many of us have ever even thought about, let alone tried to do. He then goes on to whine about the "inaccurate" and "misleading" figure of over 500 members that this forum "advertises". What kind of crapola is that?<P>Was John upset and bitter because all 500 of us didn't jump to his service the first time he ever posted a question? If so, I think John has some deep seated issues that he needs to go elsewhere to resolve.||||reg||13
Z||000019||John Smith||11-15-1999||02:37 AM||||Patrick:<BR>I came here to find a more diverse and intelligent group of individuals that I have yet to see on existing Fiero mailing lists, etc..  I did not find it to meet my expectations.  You have demonstrated the exact flameing, punk mentality that proliferates throughout the Fiero circles, and I find it rather sickening.  My comments about unregistration and the inaccurate number of members was directed at the administrators of the forum as a suggestion, and not as a comment for some ****  with a weak mind to critique. <P>Regards,<BR>         John Smith<p>[This message has been edited by John Smith (edited 11-15-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Patrick||11-15-1999||03:01 AM||||<BR>John, you've just proceeded to insult everyone involved with this forum. Why, because you couldn't get your problem attended to or diagnosed quick enough? Well, excuse us.<P>Out of respect for Cliff Pennock and this fine forum I'll refrain from further comment regarding your pathetic statements.||||reg||13
Z||000021||John Smith||11-15-1999||03:22 AM||||Why?  After a review of the posts you have made to this forum, its obvious that you are just a punk that enjoys submiting snotty remarks to others.  You have offered absolutly no input that would make you an asset to a forum such as this.  So why stop now? ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Cliff Pennock||11-15-1999||03:36 AM||||Ooooookay... Need to step in here.<P>John, there's indeed no way to unregister. The software doesn't allow for that. You'd need to ask the creators at <A HREF="http://www.ultimatebb.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ultimatebb.com</A>  why. The number of "members" on the first page was put up by request of the visitors, I never cared for it much. It's also true that it misrepresents the actual number of <I>posting</I> users (which is much lower), or the number of regular visitors (which is much higher). But I fail to understand why it is <I>misleading</I>... This forum is a free service and will always stay that way. I'm not trying to get as many members as possible or steal them away from other places, which is reflected in the fact that I don't advertise this forum on the Fiero List, nor on the Fiero newsgroup.<P>Up until now, this forum has been <B>completely</B> free of flame wars - which is quite an incredible feat considering there are over 15,000 messages here, and I (and all members) really would like it to stay that way.<P>I really find it a bit unfair to judge this forum on something <B>that simply isn't here</B>. The people on this forum have demonstrated that a discussion medium - in whatever form - <B>can</B> be free of personal flames, insults or name-calling. They have 15,000+ messages to proove that.<P>John, you have every right to judge that this forum isn't for you. And if the people here weren't able to solve your problem, I'm really sorry about that. But expect strong reactions from the members here if you criticize this forum - no matter how mild the critique. It <B>is</B> one of the few places on the net where you can discuss Fieros in a civilized manner, and that makes people very protective about it.<P>So my suggestion is: keep this forum bookmarked, visit it every now and then, read a few threads and I'm sure you'll be convinced too that this really is a fun (and educational) place to visit. A place that is able to solve <I>most</I> (if not all) of your Fiero related problems. A place where people are very forgiving for the occasional off-topic posts. And I'm equally convinced you'll come to understand Patrick's reaction. Please, don't give up so easily - you really won't regret it.<BR>||||reg||2
